  "details": "I do appreciate the steps to stop music spamming avatars, however this also harms me and my fellow avatar creators. \n\nIt's very unsettling to hear my gunfire to be lower than my voice, and all distant sounds are rather staticky, which makes them more bothersome and annoying. I used distant audio for my distant gunfire, however as that's now out of the plain of possibility, I will have to try other methods.\n\nThe thing about gunfire, is that at a distance or closerange, no matter what you do, it's going to be constantly changing db levels rapidly, so it'll be pretty hard to keep it from being compressed. I'd rather have a system where people can rate an avatar based on how malicious it is, and then this avatar will be hit with the compression system; as to how to prevent malicious intent with this system though, will be hard.\n\nFootstep audio (audio sources triggered by movement animations) removal is disappointing, but I am more disappointed in the fact that spam avatars are still loud as hell, while my gunfire (quieter than theirs) is way quieter and buggier; when it was never a problem to begin with, but theirs was.\n\nFrom experience with this update, it's actually broken many of the audio sources on people's avatars; such as non-malicious distant audio. The thing about distant audio, is that not everyone is using it for harmful purposes. I'd actually say the shaders they use are more harmful, actually. Nothing's more annoying than having your screen shake whilst seeing flashing colors.\n\nSo in conclusion, this update has done squat to prevent spam audio, and has in fact, limited avatar creativity. I hope you fix these problems, I really do.",
